# Cloudflare Pages response headers (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/).
# Every page (2026-10-23, the security audit): no other site may frame the paper (a framed page can be used to trick a
# reader into a click), no plugin content and no <base> that would re-point the page's own links, forms go only to the
# site itself, nothing the page loads may ask for the camera, microphone, location, USB or payment, and a browser
# remembers for a year to use HTTPS. Cloudflare already sends nosniff and a referrer policy; they are named here so the
# policy does not depend on its defaults. No script allowlist: the stories are already printed as text
# (src/lib/safe-url.mjs, src/lib/remark-safe-markdown.mjs), and AdSense, once on, loads scripts from many Google hosts
# that such a list would have to follow. GitHub Pages, the mirror, ignores this file.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), usb=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The fonts never change under their names, so a reader's browser keeps them for a year instead of asking again on
# every page: Tajawal is `font-display: optional` and is used only when it is there at the first paint, and with the
# default `max-age=0, must-revalidate` a slow round trip dropped it on the second page too (2026-09-27). A changed
# font file must take a new name.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
